What is Enum?
Enum means Enumeration
Used to store a fixed set of constants
Improves:
readability
type safety
maintainability

Syntax :
enum Day
{
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
}

Features of Enum

1. Enum in Switch Case :
switch(day)
{
    case MONDAY:
        System.out.println("Start");
        break;
}

2. Enum with Constructor and Methods
Enum can contain:
variables
constructors
methods

Example:

enum Level
{
    LOW(1), MEDIUM(2), HIGH(3);

    int value;

    Level(int value)
    {
        this.value = value;
    }
}

Important Enum Methods :
| Method    | Purpose                 |
| --------- | ----------------------- |
| values()  | Returns all constants   |
| valueOf() | Converts string to enum |
| ordinal() | Returns index value     |

What is Stream?
Introduced in Java 8
Used to process collections easily
Helps avoid lengthy loops

Streams are used for:
-->filtering
-->sorting
-->mapping
-->counting
-->collecting data

Creating Stream :

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Ram", "John");
names.stream().forEach(System.out::println);

Important Stream Methods :
| Method      | Purpose               |
| ----------- | --------------------- |
| filter()    | Filters data          |
| map()       | Transforms data       |
| sorted()    | Sorts elements        |
| collect()   | Stores result         |
| count()     | Counts elements       |
| limit()     | Limits elements       |
| distinct()  | Removes duplicates    |
| findFirst() | Returns first element |
| reduce()    | Performs calculations |

filter()
numbers.stream()
       .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
       .forEach(System.out::println);

-->Used to filter data based on condition.

map()
numbers.stream()
       .map(n -> n * n)
       .forEach(System.out::println);

-->Used to transform data.

sorted()
numbers.stream()
       .sorted()
       .forEach(System.out::println);

-->Used to sort elements.

collect()
.collect(Collectors.toList());

-->Stores stream result into another collection.

reduce()
int sum = numbers.stream()
                 .reduce(0, (a,b) -> a+b);

Used for:
-->sum
-->multiplication
-->aggregation operations

Advantages of Streams :
-->Less code
-->Faster processing
-->Better readability
-->Functional programming support

What is Thread?
-->A thread is the smallest unit of execution
-->Used for multitasking inside a program

Why Use Threads?
-->Perform multiple tasks simultaneously
-->Better CPU usage
-->Faster execution
-->Smooth user experience

Ways to Create Thread :

1. Extending Thread Class
class MyThread extends Thread
{
    public void run()
    {
        System.out.println("Running");
    }
}

2. Implementing Runnable Interface (Recommended)
class MyRunnable implements Runnable
{
    public void run()
    {
        System.out.println("Running");
    }
}

Reason:
-->Java does not support multiple inheritance
-->Runnable is more flexible

Thread Life Cycle :
| State           | Meaning              |
| --------------- | -------------------- |
| New             | Thread created       |
| Runnable        | Ready to run         |
| Running         | Executing            |
| Blocked/Waiting | Waiting for resource |
| Terminated      | Execution completed  |

Important Thread Methods :
| Method        | Purpose                  |
| ------------- | ------------------------ |
| start()       | Starts thread            |
| run()         | Contains logic           |
| sleep()       | Pauses thread            |
| join()        | Waits for another thread |
| setPriority() | Sets priority            |
| getName()     | Gets thread name         |

What is Wrapper Class?
Converts primitive datatype into object
Example:
int -> Integer
char -> Character

Primitive and Wrapper Classes :
| Primitive | Wrapper   |
| --------- | --------- |
| int       | Integer   |
| char      | Character |
| double    | Double    |
| float     | Float     |
| boolean   | Boolean   |

